MODULE 10.4 : QUANTUM ENTANGLEMENT

Status : In Progress

Objective:
Understand quantum entanglement and learn how two qubits can be connected through a quantum circuit.

Concepts:
• Entanglement
• Controlled-NOT (CNOT) gate
• Control qubit
• Target qubit
• Correlated measurement outcomes

Key Points:
• Entanglement is a quantum relationship between two or more qubits.
• The state of an entangled system cannot generally be described as independent states of each qubit.
• A Hadamard gate can first create superposition.
• A CNOT gate can then create entanglement between two qubits.
• In the circuit used in this module, the resulting state is approximately:
  (|00> + |11>) / sqrt(2)
• When measured many times, the expected results are approximately:
  00 → 50%
  11 → 50%
• Results 01 and 10 are not expected for this ideal circuit.
• Entanglement is an important building block of quantum computing and quantum machine learning.
• Entanglement alone does not guarantee quantum advantage.

Implementation:
Created a two-qubit Qiskit circuit using:
1. Hadamard gate on qubit 0
2. CNOT gate between qubit 0 and qubit 1
3. Measurement of both qubits
4. AerSimulator with 1000 shots

Expected Observation:
Approximately equal counts of 00 and 11.

Project Relevance:
Entanglement will later be used as part of quantum circuits for processing encoded molecular features in the Quantum Drug Discovery project.

