MODULE 10.5 : QUANTUM GATES

Status : In Progress

Objective:
Understand the basic quantum gates used to manipulate qubits.

Key Gates:

1. X Gate:
• Also called the quantum NOT gate.
• Flips the qubit:
  |0> → |1>
  |1> → |0>
• Simple meaning: bit flip.

2. Y Gate:
• Performs a bit flip together with a phase change.
• |0> → i|1>
• |1> → -i|0>

3. Z Gate:
• Does not change the computational value of the qubit.
• |0> → |0>
• |1> → -|1>
• It changes the phase of |1>.

4. Hadamard (H) Gate:
• Creates superposition.
• For an initial |0> state:
  |0> → (|0> + |1>)/sqrt(2)
• Measurement gives approximately 50% 0 and 50% 1.

5. S Gate:
• Applies a 90-degree phase rotation to |1>.
• |0> remains unchanged.

6. T Gate:
• Applies a 45-degree phase rotation to |1>.
• |0> remains unchanged.

7. CNOT Gate:
• A two-qubit controlled operation.
• First qubit is the control.
• Second qubit is the target.
• If control = 1, the target qubit is flipped.
• If control = 0, the target remains unchanged.

Easy Summary:
X → Flip
Y → Flip + phase change
Z → Phase flip
H → Superposition
S → 90-degree phase rotation
T → 45-degree phase rotation
CNOT → Controlled flip

Implementation:
Created quantum_gates.py using Qiskit.
The circuit demonstrates X, H and CNOT gates.

Project Relevance:
Quantum gates are the basic operations used to construct quantum circuits.
Later, quantum gates will be used to manipulate encoded molecular features in the Quantum Drug Discovery project.

Next Step:
